Comment: 
```
// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every valid index from this index to the old size of the list, the element at the next position is either null and the old element at this index is null, or the element at the next position equals the old element at this index.
```

We are in method add(int index, E obj). It inserts element at position index (before existing element at that index). After insertion, elements from index to old size-1 shift right by one. The spec: if index between 0 and old size inclusive, then for every valid i from index to old size-1? Actually "from this index to the old size of the list" inclusive? The original old size = \old(this.size()). After insertion size = oldsize+1. For each i in [index, oldsize] (oldsize is old size, after insertion there is new element at index, and the previous element at oldsize-1 moves to oldsize). The spec: element at next position (i+1 after insertion) is either null and old element at i is null, or element at next position equals old element at i. Means for each i in that range, this.get(i+1) equals \old(this.get(i)) unless both are null? Actually they say "either null and the old element at this index is null, or the element at the next position equals the old element at this index." That's essentially: this.get(i+1) == \old(this.get(i)) (including null case). So we can just assert equality.

But need premise: index between 0 and old size inclusive. Use \old(this.size()).

Thus assert: (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)));

Need to handle null equality: use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))) . But typical spec uses .equals which would NPE if null. Use (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

Simplify: we can use equality operator == for object references? In spec maybe they use .equals for non-null. Many examples used .equals. They didn't handle null case. In earlier example they used .equals for equality of elements. Might assume elements non-null? Not specified. But comment includes null case. So we need to include condition.

Thus:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i+1) == null ? \old(this.get(i)) == null : this.get(i+1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

Make sure parentheses.

Return final code block.

